# R2 — Interface-first design

Every cross-package boundary is defined as a **TypeScript `interface`** or
**Python `Protocol`** (a.k.a. structural type). Implementations are
**injected** (constructor / factory / DI container), never `new`-ed inline
by callers. This lets us swap real ↔ mock without touching consumers, which
is the whole reason the inspector pages can exist.

If a function reaches outside its own package and constructs something
concrete, that's a violation.

## ❌ BAD

```ts
// packages/llm-brain/src/agent.ts
import { GraphitiStore } from "@yellow-ue/memory-graph/impl/graphiti";

class Agent {
  private memory = new GraphitiStore({ url: process.env.GRAPHITI_URL! });
}
```

## ✅ GOOD

```ts
// packages/memory-graph/src/contract.ts
export interface WorldMemoryStore {
  write(fact: Fact): Promise<void>;
  read(query: Query, at?: Timestamp): Promise<Fact[]>;
}

// packages/llm-brain/src/agent.ts
import type { WorldMemoryStore } from "@yellow-ue/memory-graph";

class Agent {
  constructor(private readonly memory: WorldMemoryStore) {}
}
```

Python: same idea with `typing.Protocol` + constructor injection.

## Checklist when adding a boundary

- [ ] Define `interface` (TS) / `Protocol` (Python) in the **owning** package
- [ ] At least one real impl and one mock impl
- [ ] Consumers depend on the interface only (`import type` in TS)
